    {
      "file": "research/frontier-44-special-representation-examples-28-owner-step3-cellular-branching-bindings-round1/coherent-round1/after/ex-q-schaper-for-shape-three-two-at-e-two.md",
      "item_id": "ex-q-schaper-for-shape-three-two-at-e-two",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The DVR $R=\\mathbb C[\\hat q]_{(1+\\hat q)}$, unit $\\hat q$, uniformizer $\\pi=1+\\hat q$, residue field $F=\\mathbb C$, $q=-1$, $e=2$, and $d\\ge5$. All displayed Weyl modules are for this $S_F(d,5)$.\n\n[F1] The corrected **balanced** sum formula and its signs are [[thm-q-schaper-virtual-radical-layer-formula]] and [[def-alternating-weyl-and-specht-symbols-from-beta-sequences]]. The weight Gram product is [[thm-q-schur-weyl-weight-space-gram-determinant-hook-product]].\n\n[F2] $v([h]_{\\hat q})$ is $1$ for $h=2,4$ and $0$ for $h=1,3,5$; the even roots are simple over $\\mathbb C$. The Weyl forms have nonzero simple heads and their first reduced Jantzen submodule is the radical.\n\n[F3] The Schur functor has factor-$1$ form restriction and preserves the multiplicities of restricted simple factors ([[thm-schur-functor-sends-weyl-and-simple-modules-to-specht-and-hecke-simple-modules]]). The restricted labels among the shapes below are $(2,2,1)$, $(2,1^3)$ and $(1^5)$; $(3,2)$ and $(3,1^2)$ have zero Murphy form quotients ([[thm-simple-type-a-hecke-modules-are-indexed-by-e-regular-partitions]], [[def-type-a-hecke-specht-module-and-cell-form]]).\n\n\n\n1.1 Check the lift. Localization makes $\\hat q$ a unit since its residue is $-1$, so the exact PID hypotheses of [F1] hold; the generic factorial is nonzero. The identities $[2]=1+\\hat q$, $[4]=(1+\\hat q)(1+\\hat q^2)$ and evaluation of the odd quantum integers at $-1$ prove [F2]. These values refer to this lift, not an arbitrary field or lift with quantum characteristic $2$. [given, F1, F2]\n\n1.2 Compute the balanced symbols explicitly. For $(3,2)$, $\\beta=(6,5,3,1,0)$ and its hook array is $\\begin{smallmatrix}4&3&1\\\\2&1\\end{smallmatrix}$. The pair $(1,1),(1,2)$ gives $(9,2,3,1,0)$, which sorts to the beta sequence of $(1^5)$ with sign $-1$ and coefficient $v(4)-v(3)=1$. The pair $(1,1),(1,3)$ gives $(7,5,2,1,0)$, shape $(2,2,1)$ with sign $+1$ and coefficient $v(4)-v(1)=1$. The pair $(2,1),(2,2)$ gives $(7,4,3,1,0)$, shape $(3,1^2)$ with sign $+1$ and coefficient $v(2)-v(1)=1$. The remaining pair has repeated beta entries and vanishes. Thus the first displayed Weyl identity follows by direct substitution. [F1, F2]\n\n2.1 Compute the smaller rows rather than assuming them. The same calculation for $(3,1^2)$ gives $\\sum_{i>0}[W^{(3,1^2)}(i)]=[W^{(2,2,1)}]+[W^{(1^5)}]$: the pairs with hook lengths $(5,2)$ and $(2,1)$ give those two terms, while the $(5,1)$ coefficient is zero. For $(2,2,1)$, the only nonzero symbols arise from pairs $(4,2)$ and $(3,1)$, both of valuation difference zero. For $(2,1^3)$ the sole pair is $(5,1)$, also zero; $(1^5)$ has no pair. Hence these three Weyl modules have zero positive sums and zero radicals, so they equal their simple heads. For $(3,1^2)$ each displayed simple occurs once in the positive sum; because its first submodule is the radical and later ones lie inside it, both radical multiplicities are exactly $1$. Its class is consequently $[L^{(3,1^2)}]+[L^{(2,2,1)}]+[L^{(1^5)}]$. Substitution into step 1.2 now gives $[L^{(3,1^2)}]+2[L^{(2,2,1)}]$, proving the stated preliminary multiplicity conclusions without reading a signed individual Weyl coefficient as a simple multiplicity. [F1, F2, step 1.2]\n\n3.1 Supply the actual dimension argument. The regular-weight Gram product for the Murphy cell $(2,2,1)$ is $$\\hat q^7\\frac{[4]_{\\hat q}}{[2]_{\\hat q}}[3]_{\\hat q}^4   =\\hat q^7(1+\\hat q^2)[3]_{\\hat q}^4,$$ since its two signed symbols have shapes $(1^5)$ and $(2,1^3)$ with standard-tableau counts $1$ and $4$. The five standardizations have lengths $0,1,1,2,3$, giving exponent $7$. At $\\hat q=-1$ this determinant is $-2\\ne0$. Thus $S_{\\mathrm M}^{(2,2,1)}=D_{\\mathrm M}^{(2,2,1)}$ is simple of dimension $5$, counted by its five standard tableaux. Both Schur simples $L^{(3,1^2)}$ and $L^{(3,2)}$ are killed by the restricted-label criterion [F3]. Applying the functor to the positive sum of step 2.1 therefore gives $\\sum_{i>0}[S_{\\mathrm M}^{(3,2)}(i)]=2[D_{\\mathrm M}^{(2,2,1)}]$. The form of $(3,2)$ is identically zero by its nonrestricted label, so its first submodule is the whole five-dimensional cell. Every composition factor of that cell must appear in the positive sum, hence must be this five-dimensional simple. Dimension forces exactly one factor, proving $S_{\\mathrm M}^{(3,2)}\\cong D_{\\mathrm M}^{(2,2,1)}$. The functor's surviving-factor equality [F3] gives $d_{(3,2),(2,2,1)}=1$. [F1, F3, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 A further consistency check is the regular-weight Gram product $\\hat q^8[3]_{\\hat q}[4]_{\\hat q}^4[2]_{\\hat q}^6$ for $(3,2)$, whose valuation is $10$, agreeing with two five-dimensional positive Specht submodules. This is a check of the complete balanced formula; it is not the unsupported full-hook product used in the former classification proof. [F1, step 1.2, step 3.1]\n\n5.1 Translate simultaneously by $\\theta$ and conjugation. The actual ordinary/Murphy module-and-form comparison sends the isomorphism of step 3.1 to $S_{\\mathrm{DJ}}^{(2,2,1)}\\cong D_{\\mathrm{DJ}}^{(3,2)}$. The partition $(2,2,1)$ is not regular, so its own **cell-form quotient** is zero; this does not say its actual head is zero, since the module itself is simple. The dominance inequality $(3,2)\\unrhd(2,2,1)$ agrees with the ordinary-convention unitriangular bound. This proves every claimed numeric conclusion. [F3, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎"
    },
