    {
      "file": "research/frontier-44-special-representation-examples-28-owner-step3-cellular-branching-bindings-round1/coherent-round1/after/thm-weight-one-block-decomposition-pattern.md",
      "item_id": "thm-weight-one-block-decomposition-pattern",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** A field $F$, $q\\in F^{\\times}$ with quantum characteristic $e\\ge2$, an $e$-core $\\gamma$ of $m$, the integer $n=m+e$, and the Weyl modules $W^{\\lambda}_F$ of $S_F(d,n)$ for $\\lambda\\vdash n$ with at most $d$ parts, where $d\\ge n$.\n\n[F1] Each Weyl module has simple head $L^{\\lambda}$ of multiplicity one, the decomposition matrix is dominance-unitriangular, and all simple modules are obtained this way ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). In a finite-dimensional module, a simple head implies indecomposability: any nonzero direct summand has a nonzero head, so two nonzero summands would give a nonsimple head.\n\n[F2] The corrected balanced Jantzen formula sums positive filtration **submodules**, not their successive quotients: $$\\sum_{i>0}[W^{\\lambda}_F(i)]=\\sum_{(a,b),(a,c)\\in[\\lambda],\\ b<c}\\bigl(\\nu_\\pi([h^{\\lambda}_{ab}]_{\\hat q})-\\nu_\\pi([h^{\\lambda}_{ac}]_{\\hat q})\\bigr)[W^{\\beta+h^{\\lambda}_{ac}\\varepsilon_b-h^{\\lambda}_{ac}\\varepsilon_c}_F].$$ Here $\\beta_b=\\lambda'_b+N-b$ for a fixed sufficiently long conjugate beta sequence, and the right hand symbols are alternating symbols: sort strictly decreasing, attach the sorting sign, and vanish on repeated or negative entries. The equal transfer preserves total size. Moreover $W(1)=\\operatorname{rad}W$. This is the balanced hook-ratio formula required from [[thm-q-schaper-virtual-radical-layer-formula]] and [[def-alternating-weyl-and-specht-symbols-from-beta-sequences]]. Its source arithmetic must be repaired before binding: the manuscript's displayed beta indices are inconsistent, while its worked Example 5.28 determines the balanced transfer.\n\n[F3] Core-quotient and abacus theory: a weight-one partition is obtained from a packed core abacus by moving the bottom bead of one runner down one place. The bead move removes/adds a rim $e$-hook, and distinct runners give distinct partitions ([[def-e-core-e-quotient-and-e-weight]], [[thm-e-core-is-independent-of-the-rim-hook-removal-order]], [[def-beta-set-and-e-runner-abacus-of-a-partition]]). Restricted means $\\lambda_i-\\lambda_{i+1}<e$ for every $i$ ([[def-e-regular-and-e-restricted-partitions]]).\n\n[F4] The exact Schur functor sends $W^{\\lambda}$ to $S^{\\lambda}_{\\mathrm M}$ and $L^{\\lambda}$ to $D^{\\lambda}_{\\mathrm M}$, whose nonzero labels are precisely the restricted partitions. The ordinary Specht dictionary uses conjugation and the same $\\theta$ pullback on every module ([[def-schur-idempotent-and-schur-functor-for-type-a-q-schur-algebras]], [[thm-schur-functor-sends-weyl-and-simple-modules-to-specht-and-hecke-simple-modules]], [[def-type-a-hecke-specht-module-and-cell-form]]).\n\n\n\n**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Enumeration and ordering. Choose a conjugate core beta set long enough that every runner contains a bead, and list its bottom beads $x_1<\\cdots<x_e$. Each runner is packed, so replacing $x_k$ by $x_k+e$ gives precisely the $e$ weight-one partitions, denoted $\\lambda_k$. For $j>k$, moving $x_j$ instead of $x_k$ gives the more dominant conjugate partition: comparing the sorted bead lists, each partial sum increases when the move starts at the larger bead. Conjugation reverses dominance, hence $\\lambda_1\\rhd\\cdots\\rhd\\lambda_e$. The largest original partition is $(\\gamma_1+e,\\gamma_2,\\ldots)$, since its added horizontal first-row strip dominates every other placement. [F3, construct, algebra]\n\n1.2 Choose a lift with valuation one. If $q\\ne1$, its finite quantum characteristic $e$ is its multiplicative order, and is prime to the field characteristic; use $R=F[X]_{(X-q)}$ and $\\hat q=X$. The derivative of $X^e-1$ at $q$ is $eq^{e-1}\\ne0$, so $\\nu([e]_X)=1$; also $\\nu([h]_X)=0$ when $e\\nmid h$. If $q=1$, then $\\operatorname{char}F=p=e$. First work over $\\mathbb F_p$ with $R=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}$, $\\hat q=1$, giving $\\nu([e]_1)=\\nu(p)=1$ and $\\nu([h]_1)=0$ for $p\\nmid h$, and then extend scalars to $F$. This extension preserves the decomposition multiplicities because the q-Schur simple modules are absolutely irreducible and its integral Weyl modules commute with scalar extension. In both cases the generic factorial is nonzero and [F2] applies. [F1, F2, given, algebra]\n\n2.1 Restriction. If $\\lambda$ is not restricted, some $\\lambda_i-\\lambda_{i+1}\\ge e$, so removing the last $e$ boxes of row $i$ leaves a partition. Its size is $|\\gamma|$ and its core is $\\gamma$, so it equals $\\gamma$. Thus $\\lambda$ is obtained by adding $e$ boxes to row $i$ of $\\gamma$. An $e$-core has $\\gamma_r-\\gamma_{r+1}<e$ for every $r$, since otherwise it has a removable horizontal $e$-strip. Consequently $i>1$ is impossible: the partition inequality $\\gamma_{i-1}\\ge\\gamma_i+e$ would contradict this core property. Therefore the only nonrestricted member is $\\lambda_1=(\\gamma_1+e,\\gamma_2,\\ldots)$; it is indeed nonrestricted because its first-row difference is at least $e$. [F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 Evaluate the balanced formula. A nonzero coefficient involves a hook of length divisible by $e$. A weight-one abacus has exactly one bead-gap pair of equal residue with the gap below the bead: $x_k+e$ and $x_k$; thus its only $e$-divisible hook has length $e$. Any surviving term in [F2] replaces two bead values, and its new runner counts equal the old ones: either $e$ divides the transfer $h_{ac}$, leaving residues fixed, or $e$ divides $h_{ab}=h_{ac}+\\beta_b-\\beta_c$, exchanging their residues. Hence its sorted symbol is one of the $\\lambda_j$. For $j>k$, the distinct bead sets of $\\lambda_k$ and $\\lambda_j$ differ exactly by replacing $x_k+e,x_j$ with $x_k,x_j+e$. There is exactly one balanced transfer yielding this change: if $x_j<x_k+e$, its two hook lengths are $h_{ab}=e$, $h_{ac}=x_j-x_k$; if $x_j>x_k+e$, they are $h_{ab}=x_j-x_k$, $h_{ac}=e$. The difference $x_j-x_k$ is not divisible by $e$ since bottom beads are on distinct runners. Thus the respective unsigned coefficients are $+1$ and $-1$ by step 1.2. Sorting gives in either case the signed coefficient $(-1)^{j-k+1}$: in the packed core set, exactly one bead from each runner with bottom bead $>x_r$ lies between $x_r$ and $x_r+e$, so moving $x_r$ crosses $e-r$ beads; the relative sorting parity for the two moves, together with their opposite orientation, is $(-1)^{(e-j)-(e-k)+1}$. There are no other terms and no repeated contributions: the two lost and two gained bead values determine the pair and transfer uniquely. Therefore $\\sum_{i>0}[W^{\\lambda_k}(i)]=\\sum_{j>k}(-1)^{j-k+1}[W^{\\lambda_j}]$. [F2, F3, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 Descending induction and the matrix. For $k=e$, step 2.2 gives zero on the left; all summands are actual submodule classes with nonnegative simple multiplicities, so $W^{\\lambda_e}(1)=0$ and $W^{\\lambda_e}=L^{\\lambda_e}$. Suppose the stated rows are known for $j>k$. Substitution into step 2.2 telescopes to $\\sum_{i>0}[W^{\\lambda_k}(i)]=[L^{\\lambda_{k+1}}]$. Since $W(1)=\\operatorname{rad}W$ and each later term is a submodule of $W(1)$, positivity shows $W(1)\\cong L^{\\lambda_{k+1}}$ and $W(i)=0$ for $i\\ge2$. The head quotient is $L^{\\lambda_k}$, giving $[W^{\\lambda_k}]=[L^{\\lambda_k}]+[L^{\\lambda_{k+1}}]$. Notice that the identity $[W]=[L]+\\sum_{i>0}[W(i)]$ is used only after proving all later filtration submodules vanish; it is false for a general Jantzen filtration. Reading simple multiplicities yields the complete $e\\times e$ bidiagonal matrix. [F1, F2, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 Specht rows. Apply the exact functor of [F4] to step 3.1 and use step 2.1: $D^{\\lambda_1}_{\\mathrm M}=0$, while the other $D^{\\lambda_k}_{\\mathrm M}$ are the nonzero simples. This gives exactly the three displayed Murphy Specht class identities, hence the full $e\\times(e-1)$ Murphy matrix. Conjugation sends the core to $\\gamma'$ and the restricted simple labels to regular ones, and the same $\\theta$ pullback applied to every module preserves all composition multiplicities. Thus the ordinary Specht matrix has the same entries under the accepted conjugate/sign label dictionary. [F4, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎"
    },
    {
      "file": "research/frontier-44-special-representation-examples-28-owner-step3-cellular-branching-bindings-round1/coherent-round1/after/thm-q-schur-weyl-modules-are-in-the-same-block-iff-their-labels-have-the-same-e-core.md",
      "item_id": "thm-q-schur-weyl-modules-are-in-the-same-block-iff-their-labels-have-the-same-e-core",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** A field $F$, parameter $q$, quantum characteristic $e$, integer $d\\ge n$, and the Weyl modules in the statement. Write $S=S_F(d,n)$ and $W^{\\lambda}=W^{\\lambda}_F$.\n\n[F1] The semistandard cellular basis has cells $W^{\\lambda}$, nonzero absolutely simple heads $L^{\\lambda}$, and dominance-unitriangular decomposition multiplicities $d_{\\lambda\\mu}$ ([[def-type-a-q-schur-algebra-and-weyl-modules]]). The cellular anti-involution induces contragredient duality fixing the simple isomorphism classes, through the nondegenerate cell form on each simple head ([[lem-cell-form-is-associative-and-its-radical-is-a-submodule]], [[thm-nonzero-cell-heads-classify-the-simple-modules-of-a-cellular-algebra]]).\n\n[F2] The balanced hook-ratio Jantzen formula and alternating beta symbols ([[thm-q-schaper-virtual-radical-layer-formula]], [[def-alternating-weyl-and-specht-symbols-from-beta-sequences]]) use conjugate beta numbers $\\beta_j=\\lambda'_j+N-j$, with $N$ sufficiently large, and read $$\\sum_{r>0}[W^{\\lambda}(r)]=\\sum_{(a,b),(a,c)\\in[\\lambda],\\ b<c}\\bigl(v(h^{\\lambda}_{ab})-v(h^{\\lambda}_{ac})\\bigr)[W^{\\beta+h^{\\lambda}_{ac}\\varepsilon_b-h^{\\lambda}_{ac}\\varepsilon_c}],$$ where $W^{\\lambda}(r)$ are filtration submodules, $W^{\\lambda}(1)=\\operatorname{rad}W^{\\lambda}$, and the right hand symbols are zero on repeated or negative entries and otherwise have the sign of sorting the beta sequence. The suppliers must carry this balanced form; the earlier displayed unbalanced manuscript indices do not preserve degree.\n\n[F3] Core abaci are obtained by packing beads upwards on each runner, so their shapes depend only on runner bead counts; rim-hook removal is a bead-to-gap move ([[def-e-core-e-quotient-and-e-weight]], [[thm-e-core-is-independent-of-the-rim-hook-removal-order]], [[def-beta-set-and-e-runner-abacus-of-a-partition]]). Conjugation reverses dominance ([[def-dominance-order-on-partitions]]).\n\n[F4] Primitive central idempotents decompose the identity, and every finite-dimensional module splits into their block summands ([[def-blocks-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra-by-primitive-central-idempotents]]). Weyl classes are linearly independent in the Grothendieck group, so a nonzero accumulated Weyl coefficient in the class of a module supported in one block forces that Weyl module into the block ([[lem-virtual-weyl-combinations-detect-q-schur-block-separation]], [[lem-semistandard-weight-dimensions-detect-virtual-weyl-classes]]).\n\n\n\n**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Lift and valuation. Use $R=F[X]_{(X-q)}$ with $\\hat q=X$ and uniformizer $X-q$. The element $X$ is a unit since $q\\ne0$, and every polynomial $[m]_X=1+X+\\cdots+X^{m-1}$ is nonzero, so the generic factorial is nonzero. Its valuation $v(m)$ is positive exactly when $[m]_q=0$, equivalently $e\\mid m$ for finite $e$; for $e=\\infty$ all valuations vanish. In characteristic $p$ the positive valuation may exceed1, which causes no difficulty below. [given, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 Block support and Jantzen edges. A finite-dimensional module with simple head is indecomposable: every nonzero summand has nonzero head, so two summands would make the head nonsimple. Thus $W^{\\lambda}$ has just one nonzero block summand, and every Jantzen submodule is supported in that block. By [F4], any nonzero accumulated coefficient $J_{\\lambda\\mu}$ of $[W^{\\mu}]$ in [F2] puts $W^{\\lambda},W^{\\mu}$ in the same block. This uses the sum of submodule classes, without identifying it with the radical class. [F1, F2, F4]\n\n2.1 Balanced terms preserve the core and descend in dominance. Put $s=h_{ab}$ and $t=h_{ac}$, so $s-t=\\beta_b-\\beta_c$. If a hook coefficient is nonzero, at least one of $s,t$ is divisible by $e$. If $e\\mid t$, the shifted bead residues are unchanged. Otherwise $e\\mid s$, so $t\\equiv\\beta_c-\\beta_b\\pmod e$ and the two shifted residues exchange. Thus every surviving symbol has the same runner counts and core as $\\lambda$. Moreover replacing the two conjugate beta entries $u>v$ by $u+t,v-t$ strictly increases conjugate majorization; the sorted target therefore strictly decreases original dominance. Accumulating equal target labels preserves both conclusions. [F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 No outgoing edge implies a binary row condition. Suppose every accumulated outgoing coefficient from $\\mu$ is zero. For row $i$, put $g_i=N+i-1-\\mu_i$, a gap in its conjugate beta set; its row hooks are exactly $\\beta_j-g_i>0$. If that row contained a divisible hook $s$ and a nondivisible hook $t$, repeatedly replace $t$ by $s+t$ while their sum is another row hook. This finite climb ends with $s+t$ absent from the hook set. The balanced term for the nodes of hook lengths $s,t$ then has lower shifted bead $g_i$, which is a gap, and upper shifted bead $g_i+s+t$, which is absent from the beta set. Its symbol survives and its coefficient is $\\pm v(s)\\ne0$. There is no cancellation: any surviving balanced term replaces two lost values by a new value larger than both and a new value smaller than both, so those four values determine the pair and its row gap uniquely; the gaps $g_i$ strictly increase with $i$. This contradicts the zero accumulated coefficient. Therefore each row has either all hook lengths divisible by $e$ or none; call this the binary row condition. [F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Composition-factor cores. An off-head simple occurs in $W^{\\lambda}$ exactly when it occurs in its radical, equivalently in the positive Jantzen sum: the first submodule is the radical and every later one is contained in it. Its nonzero multiplicity in that sum implies it occurs in some $W^{\\mu}$ with $J_{\\lambda\\mu}\\ne0$, since otherwise its coefficient in the sum would be zero. Step 2.1 gives the same core and strict dominance descent. Induction on the finite dominance poset proves that every factor $L^{\\nu}$ of $W^{\\lambda}$ has $C_e(\\nu)=C_e(\\lambda)$. No identity equating the radical class with the positive sum is asserted. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n3.2 Vertical core removal under the binary row condition. A removable $e$-hook has hook length $e$ at its defining node. Every hook in that row is then divisible, so its last-column hook, a positive multiple of $e$ no larger than $e$, equals $e$. Hook lengths strictly decrease across the row, forcing the defining node to be at the row end: the strip is vertical on $e$ equal-length rows $i,\\ldots,i+e-1$ in column $j$. Removing it decreases that column height by $e$. In unaffected rows hook divisibility is unchanged. For an affected row $i+t$, hooks in the retained columns were all congruent to $-t$ modulo $e$, because row $i$ had all hooks divisible and those rows had equal length. Their new hook lengths are all congruent to $-t-1$, so the binary row condition persists. Induction shows every core-removal strip is vertical. [F3, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 The Cartan graph locally. Choose a complete orthogonal primitive idempotent system of $S$, with one representative $f_\\mu$ for each simple label and repeated representatives as necessary. Such a system lifts matrix diagonal idempotents from the split semisimple quotient: the radical is nilpotent and the iteration $u\\mapsto u+(1-2u)(u^2-u)$ squares the idempotency error, so finite iteration lifts an idempotent; successively working in complementary corners lifts an orthogonal system. Absolute simplicity gives $\\dim L^{\\lambda}f_\\mu=\\delta_{\\lambda\\mu}$, hence exact multiplication by $f_\\mu$ gives $\\dim W^{\\lambda}f_\\mu=d_{\\lambda\\mu}$. The successive cellular ideal bimodules are the contragredient cell module tensor the right cell module. Apply the exact projection $f_\\mu(-)f_\\nu$ and add their dimensions; [F1]'s duality fixes the simple labels, giving $\\dim f_\\mu Sf_\\nu=\\sum_\\lambda d_{\\lambda\\mu}d_{\\lambda\\nu}$. This is the Cartan entry of the primitive projective $f_\\mu S$. A disconnected graph on the full primitive idempotent system, with edges whenever either mixed corner is nonzero, yields central idempotents by summing each component: all mixed corners vanish in both directions. Conversely a central block idempotent forbids mixed corners. Thus simple labels in one block are joined by pairs sharing a Weyl factor, and step 3.1 proves that labels in the same block have the same core. [F1, F4, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n4.2 The minimum label and the bridge shapes. Fix core $\\gamma$ and weight $w$, and set $\\rho=(\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_l,1^{ew})$. It has core $\\gamma$ by removing its bottom vertical $e$-strips, and is dominance-minimal in the class: every same-core partition contains $\\gamma$ as a diagram, and appending all additional boxes as rows of size1 minimizes each dominance partial sum. Let $\\mu\\ne\\rho$ satisfy the binary row condition. Choose the first row $i$ with divisible hooks and put $j=\\mu_i$, $h=h^{\\mu}_{ij}=\\mu'_j-i+1$, a positive multiple of $e$. If $j=1$, every divisible hook is in column1, so step 3.2 would give $\\mu=\\rho$; thus $j>1$. Remove the vertical $h$-strip, obtaining $\\sigma$, then add $h$ boxes horizontally to its first row to get $\\nu$, and vertically to its first column to get $\\eta$. All have the same core, since $h$ is a multiple of $e$; $\\nu$ dominates $\\mu,\\eta$, and $\\eta$ is strictly below $\\mu$ because the removed nodes are moved into new bottom rows. [F3, step 3.2, construct, algebra]\n\n5.1 The bridge coefficients are nonzero. Write $l=\\ell(\\mu)=\\ell(\\sigma)$. If $i>1$, then $\\sigma_1=\\mu_1$ and the row1 hook pair in $\\nu$ at columns $j,\\mu_1+1$ has lengths $A=h^{\\mu}_{1j}$ and $h$: the first is nondivisible because row1 precedes the first divisible row, and the second is divisible. Its balanced symbol sorts to $\\mu$, so its coefficient is nonzero. If $i=1$, then $\\sigma_1=j-1$, and the row1 pair at columns $j,j+1$ has lengths $h,h-1$; its balanced transfer $h-1$ sorts to $\\mu$, again giving a nonzero coefficient. For $\\eta$, the pair at columns $1,\\sigma_1+1$ has lengths $A'=\\sigma_1+h+l-1$ and $h$. When $i>1$, $A'-h^{\\mu}_{i1}=A$, so $A'$ is nondivisible; when $i=1$, $A'=h^{\\mu}_{11}+h-1$, also nondivisible. Its balanced symbol sorts to $\\eta$. Each claimed target has precisely two lost and two gained beta values, so the uniqueness argument in step 2.2 rules out cancellation. Thus both $J_{\\nu\\mu}$ and $J_{\\nu\\eta}$ are nonzero. [F2, step 1.1, step 2.2, step 4.2, algebra]\n\n6.1 Connectivity and sufficiency. Induct upwards from $\\rho$ in the finite dominance poset. For $\\mu\\ne\\rho$, if an outgoing coefficient $J_{\\mu\\tau}$ is nonzero, then $\\tau$ is strictly below $\\mu$ in the same core class by step 2.1, so induction connects $\\tau$, and hence $\\mu$, to $\\rho$. If no outgoing coefficient exists, steps 2.2–4.4 connect $\\mu$ to $\\nu$ and then $\\eta$, with $\\eta$ strictly below $\\mu$, and induction again connects it to $\\rho$. Every such edge is a same-block implication by step 1.2. Thus every pair of labels of the same core lies in the same block. Together with step 4.1 this proves the equivalence. [step 1.2, step 2.1, step 4.1, step 2.2, step 5.1, algebra]\n\n7.1 Parameterization and infinite characteristic. Every simple is the head of a Weyl module, so every block has a partition label. The core sizes are $n-ew$, and each admissible core occurs via the partition $\\rho$ of step 4.2; step 6.1 therefore gives the stated indexing. If $e=\\infty$, all coefficients vanish by step 1.1, so positivity forces every first Jantzen submodule, hence every radical, to vanish. All Weyl modules are simple; their decomposition matrix is the identity. Step 4.1 then gives identity Cartan matrix, so every primitive projective is simple and $S$ is semisimple, with each partition its own block and core. This proves all assertions. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, step 4.1, step 4.2, step 6.1] ∎\n\nThe connectivity argument follows the corrected dominance-minimum route of Lyle–Mathas Proposition 3.7 and Corollary 4.3, while proving the needed elementary row properties locally. Their final Remark explicitly corrects the earlier first-row-only q-Schur argument; the old direct hook move can have zero coefficient."
